4.0.text:
The MPU 2026 General Election shall be conducted at the duly scheduled General Election Meeting on Sunday, November 15, 2026, in accordance with the MPU Constitution and these published Electoral Committee Rules and Procedures.

Before voting begins, the Electoral Committee shall:

Confirm the constitutional quorum for the meeting;

Confirm the final voter register and final candidate list;

Verify candidate presence in accordance with Section 4.1;

Verify the LGA designation of each eligible voter;

Prepare and secure the ballot box, tally materials, official result forms, and reconciliation records; and

Prepare the appropriate BLUE, GREEN, and YELLOW ballots for controlled issuance at the Voting Table under Section 5.4.

Election Day times are not fixed by this section. Any accreditation, opening, or closing times adopted for the 2026 election shall be announced separately by the Electoral Committee in the Election Day Notice.

Operational principle: accreditation confirms who may proceed to the Voting Table; the signed Voter Register and surrendered ticket document ballot issuance; the deposited secret ballot records the vote; and Section 6.1 reconciles those records before counting begins.

6.0.text:
When voting closes, the Electoral Committee shall:

Publicly announce that voting is closed; after that announcement, no further ballot shall be issued or accepted;

Reconcile accreditation tickets, voter-register signatures, ballots issued, ballots cast, spoiled/cancelled ballots, and unused ballots under Section 6.1;

Begin counting only after reconciliation is completed, using Sections 7.0, 7.1, and 7.3;

Apply Section 7.6 to any tie or no-majority situation; and

Record, certify, announce, and preserve the results under Section 8.0.

9.1.text:
All members remain subject to Article X of the MPU Constitution throughout the election meeting. The following conduct is prohibited:

Verbal attack, screaming, or yelling against another member while the meeting is in session;

Physical attack against another member;

Unauthorized interruption when another member has the floor;

Noise-making or other disruptive behavior during the meeting;

Fighting, physical assault, drunkenness, lewd behavior, or indecent exposure;

Malicious gossip or spreading falsehood against a member or a member's family;

Purposeful sabotage of MPU or the Executive Council;

Failure to cooperate in good faith with an authorized investigation; and

Angrily leaving the meeting in the middle of deliberation or disagreement without permission from the Executive Council.

Nothing in this section expands Article X or creates a new disciplinary offense beyond the MPU Constitution and these constitutionally authorized election-administration rules.

9.4.text:
Where conduct threatens ballot secrecy, orderly voting, election materials, reconciliation, counting, or certification, the Electoral Committee may take reasonable immediate administrative action necessary to protect the election process. Such action may include:

pausing the affected voting, counting, or reconciliation activity;

securing ballots, the ballot box, or other official election materials;

preventing duplicate voting, proxy voting, or unauthorized ballot handling;

directing an unauthorized person away from the controlled voting or counting area when necessary to stop continuing interference; and

preserving relevant records or other evidence for the official Election Day record.

These actions are administrative and protective only. This section does not give the Electoral Committee independent authority to impose a constitutional fine, suspension, termination of membership, or other disciplinary sanction.

9.5.text:
Any significant Election Day misconduct, voting-integrity concern, disruption, ballot-security incident, assisted-voting event requiring documentation, or administrative intervention shall be recorded in the Election Day Incident / Assistance / Challenge Log already required under Section 8.0 and Appendix I. The entry should include, as applicable:

date and time of the incident;

person or persons involved;

description of what occurred;

office, ballot, or election record affected, if any;

witnesses;

Immediate Electoral Committee action taken;

applicable constitutional or election-rule reference;

disposition or referral; and

initials or signatures of the presiding Electoral Committee official and one additional Electoral Committee member where practicable.

Article X, Section 2, provides that a violation of the MPU Code of Conduct may be subject to a minimum fine of $20 per violation and/or suspension, up to termination of membership. Any matter requiring such disciplinary sanction shall be referred to the constitutionally appropriate MPU body or process; the Electoral Committee shall not impose that sanction independently.
